---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";

const title = "5 Best Cinder Block Sealers (2026 Reviews & Buyer's Guide)";
const description = "Discover the best concrete and cinder block sealers to stop water leaks. In-depth reviews for basement walls, exterior foundations, and waterproofing.";

const schema = {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    headline: title,
    description: description,
    author: {
        "@type": "Person",
        name: "Alex Rivers",
    },
    datePublished: "2026-03-06",
    publisher: {
        "@type": "Organization",
        name: "The Honest Reviewers",
    },
};
---

<Layout
    title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
    description={description}
    ogType="article"
    jsonLd={schema}
    stickyCta={{
        text: "See The Top Product",
        link: "#top-pick",
    }}
>
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
        <!-- Hero Section -->
        <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
            <div
                class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
                style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
            >
            </div>
            <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
                <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
                    <TrustBadge />
                </div>
                <h1
                    class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8"
                >
                    The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Cinder Block Sealer</span> of 2026: Stop Leaks Fast
                </h1>
                <p
                    class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto"
                >
                    Whether you are waterproofing concrete block walls inside your basement or sealing an exterior foundation, an untreated cinder block will absorb water like a sponge. Here are the top-rated sealers to protect your property permanently.
                </p>
                <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
                    <a
                        href="#top-pick"
                        class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
                    >
                        See The #1 Penetrating Sealer
                    </a>
                    <a
                        href="#buying-guide"
                        class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
                    >
                        Read Waterproofing Guide
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Content -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
            <div
                class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"
            >
                <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
                    Are cinder blocks waterproof? No. Highly porous concrete and cement blocks absorb moisture rapidly, leading to efflorescence, mold, mildew, and structural deterioration. Using a highly rated concrete block water sealer is the only effective defense against elemental water damage.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A leaking basement or a deteriorating retaining wall can cause thousands of dollars in hidden water damage. Before discussing the exact chemical sealers to use, it is critical to understand the anatomy of a cinder block. Formally known in the construction industry as a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concrete_masonry_unit" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">Concrete Masonry Unit (CMU)</a>, these building blocks are fundamentally engineered to be lightweight. They are manufactured using a mixture of Portland cement, water, and various lightweight aggregates such as fly ash or bottom ash. 
                </p>
                
                <p>
                    This lightweight manufacturing process inherently creates millions of microscopic air voids throughout the structure. While excellent for insulation and construction ease, these voids turn every cinder block into a highly effective sponge. When exposed to rain or groundwater, capillary action aggressively draws water straight through the block to the interior of your basement or room.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Compounding the problem is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrostatic_pressure" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">hydrostatic pressure</a>. When the soil surrounding your home's foundation becomes saturated during a heavy rainstorm, the weight of the water physically pushes against your porous foundation walls. Finding the <strong>best concrete block sealer</strong> depends largely on your specific problem: Are you fighting back massive hydrostatic pressure inside a basement? Do you need a clear block sealer that keeps a natural architectural look for an exterior garden wall? Are you painting cinder block foundations and simply need a primer to stop moisture?
                </p>

                <p>
                    After testing various industrial and consumer products on both exterior and interior applications, we've broken down the absolute best sealers, paints, and waterproofings for cinder blocks on the market today.
                </p>

                <!-- Top Pick Highlight -->
                <div
                    id="top-pick"
                    class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden"
                >
                    <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
                        ★ Overall Best Choice
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-8 items-center">
                        <div class="w-full md:w-1/3 flex justify-center">
                            <div class="aspect-square bg-gray-800 rounded-2xl w-full flex items-center justify-center border-4 border-gray-700 shadow-xl overflow-hidden group">
                                <div class="text-xl font-black text-gray-400 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform duration-300 px-4 text-center">Siloxa-Tek 8500</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="w-full md:w-2/3">
                            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-4 border-none">Ghostshield Siloxa-Tek 8500</h2>
                            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 border-none">
                                By far the best concrete block water sealer for exterior walls. The Siloxa-Tek 8500 is an industrial-grade, clear penetrating sealer. Instead of just sitting on the surface like rubberized paint (which can eventually peel), it chemically reacts deep within the concrete's pores.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 border-none">
                                The silane and siloxane molecules in this formulation are small enough to penetrate the microscopic voids of the cinder block, bonding chemically with the silica in the cement to create an invisible, permanent hydrophobic barrier. If you want the best natural look clear block sealer, this is it.
                            </p>
                            <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-6 list-none p-0">
                                <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-bold">✓</span> 100% Breathable (Allows trapped vapor to escape)</li>
                                <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-bold">✓</span> Dramatically reduces efflorescence, mold, and mildew</li>
                                <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-bold">✓</span> Never peels, yellows, or flakes off like paint</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans">
                    The Top 5 Cinder Block Sealers Extensively Reviewed
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Depending on whether the moisture is coming from inside or outside, and whether you are fighting heavy water pressure or just light surface rain, one product will perform drastically better than another. Here are the in-depth cinder block sealer reviews for 2026:
                </p>

                <div class="space-y-12">
                    <!-- Review 2 -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-blue-100 text-blue-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Best Paint / Interior Waterproofing</div>
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">DRYLOK Extreme Masonry Waterproofer</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            If you are asking, "what is the best paint for basement cinder block walls?", DRYLOK Extreme is the undisputed king. Unlike standard exterior cinder block paint which will blister and tear under pressure, this is a heavy-duty, latex-based specialized cement block waterproofing.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            It uniquely expands as it dries to become physically part of the wall matrix. Because of this structural bond, DRYLOK Extreme is guaranteed to withstand an incredible 15 PSI of hydrostatic pressure (equivalent to a wall of water over 30 feet high). It completely seals the horrific weeping and slow drips synonymous with older block basements.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Guaranteed to stop heavy pressurized water, highly effective base coat for interior basement walls, beautifully tintable to match decor.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Changes the natural appearance to a solid painted wall, very thick consistency makes it tiring to apply with a heavy nap roller.</li>
                                <li><strong>Use Case:</strong> Waterproofing concrete block walls inside leaky, damp basements.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Review 3 -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-green-100 text-green-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Best Solvent-Based / Extreme Duty</div>
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">Foundation Armor SX5000</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            If you are sealing exterior concrete block walls or massive retaining structures exposed to severe freeze-thaw cycles or driving sideways rain, the Foundation Armor SX5000 is an incredibly potent silane-siloxane blend. 
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            It is genuinely DOT-approved (Department of Transportation) and heavily utilized in commercial bridge and highway applications. The solvent-based carrier allows the active active silane ingredients to penetrate incredibly deep into the block, providing a water-beading effect that lasts nearly a decade.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Reduces water absorption rates by up to 95%, lasts up to 10 years without reapplication, completely clear natural architectural finish.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> High Volatile Organic Compounds (requires excellent ventilation or respirators if used indoors/garages), slightly expensive.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Review 4 -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-purple-100 text-purple-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Best Eco-Friendly Option</div>
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">Seal-Once Nano+Poly Concrete & Masonry</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Featuring a unique combination of proprietary nanotechnology and polyurethane, Seal-Once deeply penetrates to form a water-resistant shield internally while simultaneously stabilizing the block's physical surface to prevent flaking and spalling. 
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            It represents a massive jump in green-chemistry. It is a completely non-toxic, zero VOC, water-based formula that cleans up effortlessly with soap and water, making it perfect for indoor applications where chemical off-gassing is a concern for families and pets.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Eco-friendly, completely safe around pets and indoor plants, highly durable against UV degradation and de-icing salt.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> The polyurethane addition means it doesn't breathe quite as well as a pure 100% siloxane formulation.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Review 5 -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Best Budget Choice</div>
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">MasonryDefender Penetrating Sealer</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            If you have a massive exterior block wall surrounding your property and need a reliable, highly cost-effective block sealant, MasonryDefender gets the job done without draining your bank account.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Utilizing a water-based siloxane formula, it provides a long-lasting architectural-grade water repellent finish that halts water intrusion fast. It is highly resistant to creating a slick or slippery surface, and it covers a surprisingly large footprint for its price point.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Unbeatable price-to-performance ratio, effortless to spray on using a cheap garden pump, clear natural look.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> May require reapplication slightly more often than premium brands (every 4-5 years instead of 8-10).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div
                    id="buying-guide"
                    class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-16 rounded-r-2xl"
                >
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
                        Buying Guide: How to Seal Cinder Block Walls Correctly
                    </h2>
                    <p class="mb-6">The biggest mistake homeowners make is buying standard "exterior masonry paint" and expecting it to stop water. Standard acrylic paint acts like a rigid plastic bag; however, cinder blocks constantly move water vapor. The vapor gets trapped behind the paint bag, causing massive blistering, bubbling, and peeling in less than a year. Here is the science you need to understand:</p>
                    
                    <div class="space-y-6">
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                                Penetrating Sealers vs. Membrane Sealers
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                                <strong>Penetrating Sealers (Silane / Siloxane)</strong> like Ghostshield or Foundation Armor act completely chemically. They soak deep into the cinder block (often up to 1/2 of an inch) and form a water-tight seal internally around the pores. These leave a 100% natural finish, cannot peel or blister because there is no surface film, and remain fully breathable, letting the block transpire water vapor out.<br><br>
                                <strong>Membrane/Film-forming Sealers (Elastomeric/Latex)</strong> like DRYLOK act as a skin over top of the block. These are superior at holding back <i>hydrostatic pressure</i> (water physically pushing against the wall) and are therefore most commonly used to waterproof basement cinder block walls from the inside out. They generally leave the block looking like it has been painted with thick, white cake frosting.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                                Interior vs. Exterior Application Directionality
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                                If you are waterproofing a bare foundation block, sealing it from the <strong>outside</strong> before burying it in dirt is always best. This prevents the block from ever absorbing water. However, if water is already leaking into an existing basement, excavating the exterior foundation is astronomically expensive (often $10,000+). In this case, <strong>waterproofing concrete block walls inside</strong> requires a negative-side pressure-resistant waterproofing paint (like DRYLOK) rather than a clear penetrating sealer, because the water is already inside the block trying to push into the room.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                                How to Seal a Block Wall Before Painting (Primers)
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                                You should never paint bare interior cinder block with standard decorative paint without a dedicated cinder block primer or a sealer first. Because the CMU is so porous, it will suck up paint aggressively, requiring 4-5 coats to look uniform while creating thousands of pinholes. Use a clear penetrating concrete sealer or a dedicated heavy block filler primer, let it cure, and then apply your high-end topcoat decorative paint.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The Complete Preparation and Application Process
                </h2>
                <p>
                    If you spray the most expensive silane-siloxane sealer directly onto a dirty, salt-covered cinder block, it will fail. Proper masonry preparation is mandatory.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Efflorescence Removal and Cleaning</h3>
                <p>
                    Have you noticed a chalky, fluffy white powder growing on your cinder block walls? This is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efflorescence" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">efflorescence</a>. It occurs when water travels through the block, dissolves the natural salts inside the cement, and carries them to the surface. As the water evaporates into the air, the dry salt crystals are left behind. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    If you apply a sealer over efflorescence, it blocks the pores and prevents penetration. You must wash this away entirely. Use a dedicated chemical efflorescence remover (which is usually a mild acid base) and a stiff bristle brush. Rinse the wall thoroughly with clean water, and crucially, let the cinder block dry completely (sometimes taking 3-4 days internally) before applying a penetrating sealer.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Structural Patching vs Sealing</h3>
                <p>
                    A clear block sealer physically cannot span gaps or fill holes. It is a liquid. If you have active weeping holes, mortar joints that have eroded away, or large lateral cracks zigzagging across your basement, you must patch them first.
                </p>
                <p>
                    For active leaks (where water is actively dripping out), use a fast-setting hydraulic cement that expands as it cures perfectly inside the hole. For non-active cracks or movement joints, use a specialized elastomeric polyurethane masonry caulk. Wait for all patching material to fully cure before moving to step three.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Correct Application Techniques</h3>
                <p>
                    <strong>For Clear Penetrating Sealers:</strong> The best tool is a standard low-pressure pump garden sprayer. Spray the wall from the bottom up, creating a horizontal line and moving upward. You want a 6-inch "rundown" of material cascading below where you are spraying. The porous block will soak it up instantly. Apply a second coat "wet-on-wet," meaning you spray the second coat before the first coat has completely dried (usually within 10-15 minutes).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>For Heavy Waterproofing Paints (DRYLOK):</strong> Do not use a sprayer. You must use a heavy 3/4-inch nap paint roller or a stiff nylon masonry brush. You must physically scrub and force the thick paint deeply into the thousands of pinholes in the cinder block. Usually, two heavy coats are required to ensure no pinholes remain, otherwise, water will find its way out.
                </p>

                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Cost Analysis: DIY vs Hiring a Pro
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Waterproofing a basement or a massive exterior retaining wall brings large variations in cost depending on who does the labor.
                </p>
                <ul class="space-y-4">
                    <li><strong>DIY Route (Interior Paint):</strong> 2 buckets of DRYLOK Extreme ($110), heavy nap rollers and brushes ($25), hydraulic cement ($15). Total cost to securely waterproof a standard 20-foot basement wall section: <strong>$150 maximum</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>DIY Route (Exterior Penetrating):</strong> 5-gallon pail of Siloxa-Tek 8500 ($230), cheap garden pump sprayer ($20). Covers roughly 500-750 square feet. Total cost: <strong>$250</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Professional Basement Waterproofing:</strong> Hiring a company to come in and address weeping cinder block walls generally starts at $2,000 to $6,000 for interior barrier applications, and can easily exceed $10,000+ if exterior excavation of the foundation is required.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Frequently Asked Questions
                </h2>
                
                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best way to seal cinder block walls?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">The absolute best method depends entirely on your desired finish. For a natural architectural look that won't ever peel or blister, apply two wet-on-wet coats of a Silane/Siloxane penetrating sealer using a garden sprayer. For severe basement leaks subjected to hydrostatic pressure, use a heavy nap roller to physically force a latex waterproofing paint like DRYLOK into every pore of the mortar and block.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do concrete blocks absorb water?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, extremely rapidly. A cinder block is made of Portland cement, water, and aggregates designed specifically to be lightweight, which leaves millions of tiny air pockets inside the matrix. Without a high quality sealer, it acts exactly like a hard sponge, drawing humidity and liquid water straight through to the other side via capillary action.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How to seal a painted cinder block wall?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">You physically cannot apply a breathable penetrating sealer over a previously painted wall because the paint resin will prevent the siloxanes from penetrating into the silica of the cement. If the wall is already painted, you must either mechanically/chemically strip the paint completely off back to bare masonry, or you must use another coating-type sealer (like another layer of specialty waterproofing elastomeric paint) applied directly over the existing, well-adhered paint layers.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Where to buy cinder blocks in bulk?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">If you are starting a new project or building a raised garden bed, you can buy standard 8x8x16 cinder blocks at major home improvement stores like Home Depot, Lowe's, or Menards. However, if you are purchasing more than a single pallet, always source them directly from a local masonry supply yard or concrete plant to avoid massive retail markups on delivery fees.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How to waterproof a cinder block pool?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">A subterranean pool requires immense hydraulic pressure resistance that must work while entirely submerged. Do not use standard consumer sealers. You must aggressively parge the rough blocks with a specialized hydraulic cement scratch coat and finish with a heavy-duty 2-part pool epoxy, or a rubberized liquid membrane specifically rated for permanent full-time underwater submersion.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-16 text-center bg-blue-50 p-12 rounded-3xl border border-blue-100 shadow-inner">
                    <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                        Stop Leaks Before They Cause Real Damage
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto">
                        Cinder block water damage can compromise your home's structural foundation and permanently ruin a finished basement. Choose a high-quality, reputable product like Siloxa-Tek or DRYLOK to seal your walls permanently today.
                    </p>
                    <a
                        href="#top-pick"
                        class="inline-block px-10 py-5 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-2xl shadow-xl shadow-[#FF4500]/30 transition-all transform hover:-translate-y-1 active:scale-95 no-underline text-xl"
                    >
                        Review Our #1 Block Sealer Recommendation
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </article>
</Layout>
